{
 "lead": "Active control technology (ACT) makes the flight control computers do more than follow the stick: they shape airframe loads and supply stability. Relaxing static stability lets the centre of gravity move aft, so the tail pushes down less and trim drag falls. Load alleviation cuts gust and manoeuvre peaks, so the wing can be lighter. Some syllabi call such an aircraft a control configured vehicle; some leave the topic out. Load alleviation flies on several modern airliners, and relaxed stability is standard on modern fighters.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#digital-fly-by-wire-and-fly-by-light\">the chapter on digital fly-by-wire</a> for the fly-by-wire chain and <a href=\"#autopilot-and-automatic-flight-control\">the chapter on autopilots</a> for closed-loop thinking.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Relaxed static stability",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A conventional aircraft is stable in pitch because its centre of gravity (CG) lies ahead of the neutral point, the point where a change in angle of attack gives no pitching moment. The gap is the static margin. A positive margin means the tail must push down in steady flight."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "SM = (x<sub>np</sub> − x<sub>cg</sub>) ÷ c̄",
     "where": "SM is the static margin (dimensionless); x<sub>np</sub> is the neutral point position, x<sub>cg</sub> the centre of gravity position, both measured along the chord from the same datum in metres; c̄ is the mean aerodynamic chord in metres. SM > 0 means statically stable."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "That download is lift the wing makes and the tail throws away, and it grows with the margin. ACT lets the designer shrink the margin and push the CG aft, because the computer now supplies pitch stability artificially. Download falls, trim drag falls, fuel burn improves, and the tail can be smaller."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The price: the aircraft cannot be flown without the computer, since the bare airframe diverges slowly in pitch. Losing the computer is a design case that must be shown safe. This is relaxed static stability (RSS). In a transport the margin is cut to near zero to save trim drag. In a fighter such as the F-16 the margin is made negative on purpose, so the aircraft pitches into a turn more readily, and the computer keeps it controllable."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Load alleviation and ride quality",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The same loop does load alleviation. Gust load alleviation (GLA) senses vertical acceleration with accelerometers near the wing tips and in the fuselage. A band-pass filter keeps only the band of frequencies at which gusts shake the aircraft and bend the wing. It rejects the steady 1 g part, so a steady climb passes through untouched, and it rejects high-frequency sensor noise. The computer then deflects ailerons and spoilers to unload the wing as the gust arrives, cutting the peak bending moment at the wing root."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Manoeuvre load alleviation (MLA) does the same for the pilot's pull: when pitch rate shows a manoeuvre starting, the surfaces shift lift inboard and the root bending peak falls."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Flutter suppression uses the sensing too. Flutter is an unstable oscillation from bending and torsion coupling. The surface is driven so its aerodynamic force is in phase with the flutter mode's velocity (90° out of phase with its displacement), which adds damping. Ride smoothing cuts the response in turbulence. Envelope limiting caps the pilot's demand so angle of attack α, load factor n and dynamic pressure q stay inside the envelope — carefree handling."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Hardware and the certification argument",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The chain is short: accelerometers and rate gyros (see aircraft transducers) feed the computers, which shape the command and pass it to the same actuators that fly the surface. The band-pass filter is what separates ACT from the basic control law. Actuator rate limits matter: a surface that cannot move fast enough lags the command, adds phase, and can drive a mode unstable instead of damping it."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Certification rests on two demonstrations. The function must be shown not to make any load worse anywhere in the envelope, because a wrong deflection at the wrong moment is itself a load case. Its failure must be shown safe, with the airframe surviving the function switched off."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The prize is structural mass: where gust load sizes the wing root and the wing is certified to the reduced peak load, the bending material there shrinks, and that saving is carried for the life of the aircraft."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "figure": {
  "after_section": 2,
  "caption": "Wing load factor after a gust at 1 s (computed damped responses, period 1 s). Without active control the peak is 2.5 g; with gust load alleviation it is 2.0 g and the ringing dies faster. Multiplying by 1.5 gives the ultimate design loads of the worked example.",
